  Dark Cinematic CGI = Dark cinematic CGI — high‑contrast, moody realism with dramatic lighting and filmic color grading. Key elements: Lighting: strong directional key (low‑angle or top‑side), deep hard shadows, selective rim/backlight for separation, sparse fill to preserve contrast; motivated practical lights and subtle negative fill. Tones & Grade: desaturated midtones, crushed blacks, muted highlights; teal‑orange or cool‑desaturated teal with warm accent highlights; lift‑gamma‑gain adjustments, subtle film grain, and bloom on brightest sources. Materials & Surfaces: physically based materials with layered roughness; believable wet or oily speculars, worn edges, subtle microdetail and grime; accurate fresnel and anisotropy for metals. Atmosphere & FX: volumetric fog/shafts, dust particles, smoke, and subtle godrays; screen‑space ambient occlusion and contact shadows. Cinematography: low or Dutch angles, shallow depth of field with cinematic bokeh, tight framing, dramatic negative space; camera lens artifacts (chromatic aberration, lens flares, slight vignetting). Rendering & Post: high sample counts, temporal/denoised GI or baked lighting, LUT‑based color grading, filmic response curve, grain, dodge & burn selective contrast, and local color grading for mood. Mood & Detail: gritty, tense, and immersive — emphasis on realism, tactile detail, and cinematic storytelling through light and shadow.
