  Chibi sticker = Style: Contemporary digital cartoon with heavy anime/kawaii influence — playful, cute character art. Linework: Clean, slightly sketchy black outlines with varied weight (thicker for outer contours, thinner for inner details) giving energy and emphasis. Color: Pastel-dominated palette with high saturation in accents; flat fills with light cell-shading and occasional soft gradients. Rendering: Simple cel-shading plus small highlights and blush spots to suggest volume without realism. Proportions & anatomy: Stylized/chibi-adjacent — oversized head, short torso and limbs, exaggerated facial features (large eyes, small nose, expressive mouth). Composition: Waist-up portrait, centered figure, clear silhouette and minimal background to keep focus on character. Overall mood & use: Cheerful, whimsical, character-driven illustration suited for avatars, stickers, webcomics, or social-media art.
  Line cute sticker = (a Line style cute sticker:1.5). white borders.
  Anime Sticker = (an Anime style Sticker:1.5). white borders.
  Disney 3D Sticker = (a Disney 3D style Sticker:1.5). white borders.
  Vintage Sticker = (in Vintage Sticker style, Sticker from the 1960s:1.5). white borders.
  Flaticon = (small stars:1.4), Flat, geometric vector illustration with thick, uniform outlines (bold stroke). Simplified, icon-like characters using rounded shapes and minimal facial detail (dots for eyes, curved mouths). Bright, solid color fills with subtle, flat shading blocks to suggest form without realism. Playful, friendly aesthetic typical of modern UI/marketing illustrations and emoji/icon sets. Symmetrical, balanced composition with clear visual hierarchy (subject foregrounded). Clean, scalable design optimized for screens and small sizes.
  Flaticon 2 = Minimal, geometric line‑icon style: clean, uniform stroke weight defines form with rounded terminals and simplified silhouette; shapes are reduced to essential contours for instant legibility. Two‑color palette with flat fills: limited to black outlines and a warm color as the sole accent color; fills are flat (no gradients), creating strong contrast and a modern, friendly look. Consistent visual language: same corner radii, stroke thickness, and dot sizes ensure cohesion. Playful, slightly organic details: curved lines, soft corners, and simplified anatomical hints give the subject a friendly, approachable character rather than realistic rendering. Usability-focused design: clarity at small scale, strong silhouette recognition, and restrained color usage make it suitable for UI, educational graphics, or packaging.
  Flaticon 3 = Flat vector illustration with thick, even outlines (bold stroke) around shapes. Simplified, geometric facial features: circular eyes, curved mouth, minimal detail — a friendly, cartoon-like emoji style. Limited, high-contrast color palette with solid fills and subtle inner shading (soft highlights and thin inner stroke) to suggest depth without realism. Smooth, rounded forms and uniform line weight give a playful, accessible look typical of modern UI/iconography. Decorative accent (hair highlight, striped top) for character and distinction while keeping overall minimalism. Overall aesthetic: clean, flat-design avatar/icon — optimized for clarity at small sizes and digital interfaces.
  Flaticon 4 = Flat vector illustration (digital): clean, crisp shapes with perfectly even color fills and no visible brush texture. Minimal, geometric construction: head and body built from simple ovals and rounded rectangles; facial features reduced to basic shapes (small rounded eyes, simple curved nose, subtle smile). Uniform, thin-to-medium stroke absence: outlines are either very thin or omitted entirely, relying on color contrast and silhouette instead of linework. Limited, harmonious color palette: muted pastels and desaturated tones for a friendly, non‑photorealistic look. Subtle, economical shading: small, slightly darker fills used sparingly at neck, under hairline, and cheeks (soft blush) to imply volume while preserving flatness. Smooth, rounded forms and proportions: soft curves, symmetrical composition, and slightly oversized head relative to shoulders for an approachable, avatar-like appearance. Iconographic clarity: high legibility at small sizes—features simplified to read easily in UI contexts (profiles, contact lists, illustrations). Contemporary editorial/stock-art sensibility: polished, neutral character design intended for broad, non-specific demographic use (generic, friendly, inclusive).
  Flaticon 5 = Flat vector / glyph-style illustration: solid, uniform color fills with no texture. Thick, even outlines and smooth, simplified contours defining hair, face, and clothing. Minimal, geometric facial features: small eyes, simple brows, tiny nose and mouth—reduced to essential shapes. Limited, muted, harmonious palette used in large flat planes for strong silhouette. Subtle internal shading via slightly darker fills (neck and under hair) to imply depth while keeping overall flatness. Symmetrical, icon-ready composition centered on the head and shoulders for immediate legibility at small sizes. Overall aesthetic: modern UI/avatar icon — clean, friendly, and optimized for clarity in interfaces or sticker packs.
