  Lisa Frank = in Lisa Frank art style, rainbow colors, neon accents, psychedelic patterns, 1990s school supplies aesthetic, cute holographic effects.
  Adrian Tomine = in Adrian Tomine's illustration in The New Yorker style. Core look: Clean, understated cartooning blending comic-strip clarity with literary realism; restrained, observational scenes focused on everyday urban life. Line & drawing: Precise, economy of line often likened to ligne claire—clear contour lines, minimal hatching, careful proportions; figures are realistic but simplified. Color & tone: Flat, muted palettes and subtle color shifts; limited chroma creates a quiet, contemplative mood. Composition & storytelling: Compositional clarity and strong negative space; sequences emphasize pacing, timing, and small emotional beats—stories are character-driven, focusing on awkwardness, isolation, and interpersonal nuance. Lettering & panel work: Clean, legible lettering integrated into page rhythm; panel layouts are conventional but precise, serving characterization and silent beats as much as dialogue. Influences & feel: Combines alternative comics sensibilities with commercial illustration—intimate, literate, wry, and emotionally restrained.
  Sachin Teng = in Sachin Teng's surreal and fantasy editorial magazine illustration style. Visual synthesis: digital illustration that Blends digital painting with vector-like precision; combines painterly textures, clean geometric shapes, symbolic interiors, layered narrative elements, and crisp linework. Color: bold, dark muted palettes that favor warm earth tones and jewel-like accents; controlled contrasts that push focal points, while backgrounds use muted or textured washes. Composition: Layered, collage-like arrangements where foreground figures interact with abstract shapes and negative space; dynamic diagonals and balanced asymmetry. Subjects & themes: Figurative work (often solitary characters) merged with surreal, symbolic elements—flora, celestial motifs, mechanical fragments, masks, and surreal juxtapositions that merge the literal and symbolic—suggesting narrative, myth, or inner states. Line & rendering: Fine, controlled line work combined with painterly, textured digital brushstrokes; figures and faces are often smoothly rendered with minimal lighting and deep shadows while surrounding shapes may be more graphic or blocky. Texture & detail: Subtle grain, paint strokes, and noise give tactile depth despite digital execution; fine illustrative details (hair, facial features, botanical elements) contrasted with simplified blocks. Mood & tone: Elegiac, slightly uncanny; mixes melancholy and wonder—emotive but restrained rather than overtly dramatic. Influences & references: Feels like a hybrid of contemporary editorial illustration, pop-surrealism, and classical portraiture—references to comics, animation design, and fine-art composition (comparisons often drawn to James Jean). Typical techniques: Digital layering, masked shapes, clipping for silhouettes, custom brushes for organic texture, and careful color grading to unify disparate elements.
  Jim Lee = (in Jim Lee comics art style:1.4)
